## What I did this session

Continued #1 at step 4a — fixing the two mount-time purges that destroy the authority manifest, which must land before the replay gate (step 5) can be turned on.

**Found a defect in the previous session's own design.** Sess51 planned to "purge our retained EX/PW bits" at post-recovery settle. That's wrong: by settle time some of those bits have been legitimately re-adopted by the live mount (retained EX on AG=3 → `xlog_recover` acquires it → FS sets `pag_dlm_cached=true`). A blanket purge strips the on-disk bit while the FS still thinks it holds the AG — which manufactures exactly the publish-without-authority failure we already have open as defect #2.

**GPT review (RULE 5)** approved the adoption approach but rejected my proposed discriminator as insufficient, and named a fatal race: acquire reads the retained bit → settle clears it → acquire tracks and returns success → the node holds a local fiction. Resolution: make adoption *CAS* (gated to the mount window) so adopt and settle compete on the same slot and the loser re-reads. No global lock — an rwlock would be held across the 8-minute acquire hardcap and deadlock the mount.

Three of GPT's audit items resolved by reading code: the BAST callbacks aren't registered until after `xfs_mountfs`, so no BAST can revoke authority during recovery at all; ICLUSTER is inert (`mxfs_icluster_dlm` defaults 0, never acquired); and `single_node` holds are deliberately never written to disk, so purging can't strip one.

**Shipped:** `dlm/dlm_caw.{c,h}` — `mxfs_dlm_caw_purge_dead_nodes_ex()` with `KEEP_EX` / `SKIP_TRACKED`, the `is_tracked_held()` discriminator, an explicit block on `caw_tombstone_slot` under `KEEP_EX` (it zeroes `ex_grant_epoch`), and the adopt-window state. Build is clean. No caller uses the new flags yet, so runtime behavior is unchanged from 0.11.398 — a safe intermediate state.

Design, ruling, and the remaining 6-item edit list for 0.11.399 are in ccmemory as `ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess52-GPT-ruling-adopt-window-and-settle-race`.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 11 open defects, 4 critical.

